Design: the worker no longer knows how to manage voices — it resolves the
provider from op.profile (None = the worker's default, preserving the pre-multi-
engine callers) and calls voice_op, so each profile owns its catalog: VieNeu
keeps the SDK registry (add/remove + persist_voices + denoise at 44.1 kHz, i.e.
byte-identical behavior and terminal payloads), Qwen Base enrolls/removes in the
profile-scoped clone store, CustomVoice rejects with the capability reason.
VoiceOp deliberately carries a bare profile rather than a SynthesisContext: a
voice op has no language/voice/generation identity, and jobs.SynthesisJob.context
keeps its documented "None for voice/warmup ops" invariant. Enrollment data
(transcript/consent) rides on VoiceOp but is only *enforced* by the store, which
is what keeps the capability table the single source of truth (the provider
cannot bypass the consent rule even by accident — covered by a test). Qwen
remove accepts a clone id or a display name resolved strictly inside its own
profile, which is the isolation the spec asks for and is covered both at the
store and provider level. The runtime prompt is never persisted: every host
generation receives voicePrompt/refText again and rebuilds/caches the prompt
inside the child (host-level cache tests live in test_qwen_host.py).
